WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 1.6.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:03.520
Scam defence.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.297
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:07.387
be able to recognise the common attacks

00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:08.955
before they cost you anything.

00:00:10.255 --> 00:00:11.545
Here's the plan.

00:00:11.545 --> 00:00:13.230
First, the key ideas.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.945
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:17.470
Then, your checklist.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.632
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:20.932 --> 00:00:24.315
Phishing site, Look-alike URL / sponsored search

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:26.687
ad / fake app, Bookmarks only.

00:00:26.687 --> 00:00:29.382
Never click links from DMs or ads.

00:00:29.382 --> 00:00:32.336
Wallet drainer, You sign an approval, Permit or

00:00:32.336 --> 00:00:35.897
setApprovalForAll for an attacker, Read every prompt.

00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:37.812
Burner wallet for new sites.

00:00:37.812 --> 00:00:41.921
Address poisoning, Attacker sends $0 transfers from an address

00:00:41.921 --> 00:00:44.726
that looks like one you use, so it appears

00:00:44.726 --> 00:00:48.352
in your history, Never copy addresses from history.

00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:49.741
Use a saved address book

00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:51.542
and check the full address.

00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:54.523
Fake token / airdrop, Unknown tokens appear in

00:00:54.523 --> 00:00:57.677
your wallet with a "claim" link, Ignore them.

00:00:57.677 --> 00:00:59.672
Don't interact or try to sell.

00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:04.631
Impersonation, "Support", "admin" or "project team" DMs you, Real

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:08.312
teams never DM first and never ask for seeds.

00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:12.155
Too-good yields, New protocol, huge APY, anonymous

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:15.545
team, no audit, Due diligence (Module 6).

00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:17.850
Burner wallet and small size, or skip.

00:01:19.151 --> 00:01:21.666
Let's work an example,

00:01:21.666 --> 00:01:24.581
step by step. You regularly send

00:01:25.281 --> 00:01:26.876
to 0x7a3F…9c21. Today

00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:31.026
your history shows a transfer from 0x7a3F…9c21, but

00:01:31.726 --> 00:01:34.966
the full address is 0x7a3Fe0b4…d189c21: same

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:37.486
start and end, different middle. If

00:01:38.186 --> 00:01:40.723
you copy it from history

00:01:40.723 --> 00:01:43.256
you send your funds to

00:01:43.256 --> 00:01:47.076
the attacker. Always paste from your address

00:01:47.076 --> 00:01:50.316
book and check the full address.

00:01:51.616 --> 00:01:53.076
Here's your checklist.

00:01:53.076 --> 00:01:54.611
Do it now, for real.

00:01:54.611 --> 00:01:57.630
First, Protocol sites bookmarked; never reached

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.566
through search ads or DMs.

00:01:59.566 --> 00:02:01.120
Next, Address book in use;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.491
full address checked before sending.

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:05.706
Then, Unknown tokens ignored.

00:02:05.706 --> 00:02:09.796
Also, DMs from "support" treated as scams by default.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.641
And finally, New sites only with the burner wallet.

00:02:13.941 --> 00:02:15.151
Question 1.

00:02:15.151 --> 00:02:17.822
A new token worth "$5,000" appears in

00:02:17.822 --> 00:02:20.230
your wallet with a claim site.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:21.080
What do you do?

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.315
The answer: Ignore it.

00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:30.325
It's bait, and interacting risks a drainer approval.

00:02:31.624 --> 00:02:32.869
Question 2.

00:02:32.869 --> 00:02:34.964
How does address poisoning trick people?

00:02:39.384 --> 00:02:41.995
The answer: It puts a look-alike address in

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.309
your history so you copy it by mistake.

00:02:45.609 --> 00:02:46.879
Question 3.

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.284
What's the single best habit against drainers?

00:02:53.704 --> 00:02:57.036
The answer: Reading every signature and approval prompt,

00:02:57.036 --> 00:02:59.549
and using a burner wallet for anything new.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:03.364
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:03.364 --> 00:03:08.504
Next up, Lesson 1.7: Reading signatures and simulating transactions.
